{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1583749884/11/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2601afb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nRotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.\n- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.\n\nRule\n- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.\n- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.\n- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.\n\nKey insight\n- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:\n  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3cd86f4f\" lines=\"6-18\">\n<description>\nShear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.\n\nRule\nCreate a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy\neach input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.\n\nKey insight\nThe colors and row contents are preserved; only the row offsets change so\nthe left edge forms a descending diagonal.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4941b18\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMove red into teal's top marker position, then move teal to the bottom outside corner of the grey rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds one filled rectangular non-background block.\n- There is exactly one red marker and one teal marker, both in the row immediately above the rectangle.\n- Both markers are horizontally aligned over columns inside the rectangle.\n\nRule\n- Clear the original red marker.\n- Recolor the original teal marker cell red.\n- Place the teal marker beside the rectangle's bottom row, on the side indicated by the horizontal direction from red to teal.\n\nKey insight\n- The red-to-teal horizontal order chooses the rectangle side where teal exits at the bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"13f06aa5\" lines=\"36-51\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored marker from its directional sprite to a grid edge, painting a dotted ray and the whole edge side.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One repeated body color forms small 8-connected directional sprites.\n- Each marker is a singleton non-background, non-body color embedded in one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- The body pixels around a marker lie mostly behind it; the opposite direction is the marker's ray direction.\n- Paint every second pixel from the marker along that ray, then paint the entire boundary side reached by the ray.\n- If two painted boundary sides meet at a corner, make that shared corner black.\n\nKey insight:\n- The same seven-pixel body sprite rotates to encode the direction; its local center-of-mass bias points away from the ray.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"60d73be6\" lines=\"22-39\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse colored marker pattern by reflecting it across a full-row/full-column cross.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background and one non-background cross color that\nfills exactly one complete row and one complete column. All other foreground\npixels are sparse markers away from that cross.\n\nRule\nKeep the input unchanged, and for each marker pixel copy its color to the\npositions obtained by reflecting it across the cross row, across the cross\ncolumn, and across both axes. Reflected positions outside the grid are\nclipped.\n\nKey insight\nThe full cross is not a copied object; it is the pair of mirror axes for\ncompleting the quadrant pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9aaea919\" lines=\"38-51\">\n<description>\nTransfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.\n- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.\n- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.\n- All marker bars are removed.\n\nKey insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1be83260\" lines=\"41-58\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored slot map inside the marked motif and rebuild the motif lattice with those colors.\n\nInput structure:\n- A blank background surrounds a rectangular lattice of identical motifs.\n- The motif rows and columns are separated by one background line.\n- One motif has colored marker pixels in its internal odd-row/odd-column slots.\n\nRule:\n- The motif size is 2R+1 by 2C+1, where R by C is the motif lattice size.\n- Read the marked motif's odd internal slots as the color map for the R by C output motifs.\n- Slots that still contain the repeated motif color map to that repeated motif color.\n- Rebuild the compact lattice on a canvas filled with the repeated motif color,\n  painting each motif mask with its decoded color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The holes and filled odd slots inside one motif are a miniature legend for recoloring the entire repeated motif lattice.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"cc9053aa\" lines=\"59-72\">\n<description>\nRecolor the unique safe route between the two maroon endpoints from teal to maroon.\n\nInput structure\n- A black background contains a teal 4-connected track, exactly two maroon endpoint pixels, and one or more orange blocker pixels embedded near the track.\n\nRule\n- Treat each orange blocker and its 8-neighborhood as forbidden.\n- In the remaining teal/maroon track graph, there is exactly one simple\n  4-connected path between the maroon endpoints.\n- Recolor teal pixels on that path to maroon; leave endpoints, blockers, and all other cells unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The orange pixels block nearby teal cells diagonally as well as orthogonally, which removes shorter decoy routes and makes the intended endpoint-to-endpoint path unique.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e453dd6\" lines=\"39-52\">\n<description>\nSlide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then shoot red rays from enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A vertical grey (5) separator column divides the grid into left and right halves.\n- Black shapes sit on the left side against a background color (bg).\n\nRule:\n- Translate each black connected component rightward until its right edge is flush with the separator.\n- After sliding, find enclosed bg regions (holes) within the black shapes that touch the column just left of the separator.\n- For each row spanned by such a hole, emit a red ray across the entire right half of the grid.\n\nKey insight: Only holes adjacent to the separator trigger rays — the sliding creates enclosures that act as \"nozzles\" projecting rightward.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"da515329\" lines=\"11-24\">\n<description>\nGrow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each arm tip launches a \"turtle\" heading 90° right of its outward direction.\n- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).\n- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.\n- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.\n\nKey insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1ae2feb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nExtend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.\n- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.\n- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.\n- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.\n- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its\n  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.\n\nKey insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"aa4ec2a5\" lines=\"52-66\">\n<description>\nAdd red outlines around objects, and mark enclosed holes inside hollow objects.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly uniform background contains one or more 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Some objects may enclose 4-connected background holes inside their bbox.\n\nRule:\n- For every foreground component, draw a one-cell 8-neighborhood outline in red (2).\n- If a component has enclosed background holes, recolor the component to teal (8).\n- Fill those enclosed holes with magenta (6) before drawing the outline.\n\nKey insight: Holes are treated as part of the object for outlining, while only objects\nthat actually enclose holes get the teal/magenta interior recoloring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"396d80d7\" lines=\"18-30\">\n<description>\nPaint the inner foreground color into every background pixel that touches the enclosing frame only diagonally.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color is an enclosing frame: its bbox strictly contains the bbox of the other, inner color.\n\nRule:\n- Keep all existing pixels unchanged.\n- For each background pixel, if it is diagonally adjacent to the frame color and has no orthogonal adjacency to the frame color, recolor it to the inner color.\n\nKey insight: The inner color marks the color to use, while the frame's diagonal-only background neighbors define the cells to add.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db615bd4\" lines=\"111-130\">\n<description>\nThicken the sparse frame and stamp the external sparse sprites into it in positional order.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a regular lattice color, one large\nsparse rectangular frame, and one or more external monochrome sprites whose\npixels also sit on a two-step lattice.\n\nRule\nUse the largest sparse rectangular perimeter as the frame. Clear that frame\nregion and the external sprite pixels to background, draw a solid one-pixel\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
